[2026-06-07T16:21:25.363Z] [INFO] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_536","type":"reasoning","text":"**Preparing PR body**\n\nI need to prepare the PR body, and it should include a screenshot of the future visualization with a link. I also need to mention the updated test counts: the full Cypress passed 59 tests before; now, it shows 18 after. It seems like we can say, “npx cypress run (59 passing, before the final token-disable patch; final targeted pipeline rerun 18 passing).\" \n\nI want to keep the PR body concise but accurate. The final JS patch means no full Cypress rerun occurred, but it was executed before the last small patch.\n**Considering Cypress rerun**\n\nIf I just mention \"npx cypress run\" without clarification, it could be misleading. I could rerun the full Cypress after the final patch for accuracy since it takes about 2 minutes, and we do have time. But wait, I've already pushed the commit. If I rerun and commit the new log, that could work, but I’d be committing logs without code changes.\n\nMaybe it’s cleaner to not commit the logs and just state, \"Full Cypress ran before the final visual changes and targeted pipeline rerun after\". Given the user’s instruction for \"all tests locally,\" running the full tests before and after the final patch might be sufficient.\n**Deciding on full Cypress run**\n\nRunning a full Cypress test after the final patch would ensure rigorous testing. Since it costs about 2 minutes, I wonder if the default timeout is also 2 minutes. The user mentioned not to set a custom timeout, but last time it was around 1:53 — so it might be tight. If it exceeds, the session continues anyway. I'll run full Cypress but probably won't commit the logs unless necessary. I could just redirect to an ignored timestamp to keep things tidy in the worktree."}}
